{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST be the home of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The only structural obligation in `requirements.cli.skill_structure.bootstrap` is that the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill be the home of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code. Under `requirements.cli.skill_structure`, each topic must be stated in full in exactly one skill.\n\nThe supplied root is `assets/skills/habeas-bootstrap-requirements/SKILL.md`, the whole file. It does carry this topic. Its front matter says it inventories tests, Rust items and behavior, and proposes an initial requirement set. Its body has `section[Bootstrap%20requirements]` with the subsections Workflow, Inventories, Proposing requirements, Tagging, Report and Gotchas. The introduction says \"Bootstrapping derives requirements from code that already exists\".\n\nA text search of `assets/skills` for \"bootstrap\" and \"initial requirement\" found no other skill stating the topic. `assets/skills/habeas/SKILL.md` only routes adoption work to this skill, in `section[Habeas]/section[Workflow]`. That is a pointer, not a second full statement.\n\nThe tool output elided most of the SKILL.md prose, so my conclusion rests on the outline, the headings and the search excerpts. I found no structural gap and no missing root.\n\nbehavioral: The subject has one behavioral obligation. Under `requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST be the home of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code. Home means the topic is stated in full there and in no other skill.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_bootstrap_skill_is_home_of_deriving_an_initial_requirement_set]` is marked `{test}` and covers both halves of that obligation:\n\n- **Stated in full in the bootstrap skill.**\n  - It asserts the `SKILL.md` has the sections `## Inventories`, `## Proposing requirements`, `## Tagging` and `## Report`. The `section` helper panics if a heading is missing.\n  - It asserts specific content in those sections. Proposing: \"Write one requirement per obligation a user could rely on\" and \"Label each proposal and act on the label\". Tagging: `{test}` on tests and `{impl}` on roots, and mapping each other inventoried item to the requirement whose root reaches it. Report: the fields `Tests mapped:`, `Items mapped:`, `Independent recount:` and `Gaps:`.\n- **Stated in no other skill.**\n  - It calls `assert_home(\"habeas-bootstrap-requirements\")`. This asserts the skill's whole text (`SKILL.md` plus references) contains the home markers: \"every first-party runnable test or test case\", \"## Proposing requirements\" and \"Independent recount:\".\n  - It also asserts that none of the other four skills (`SKILLS`) contains any of those markers.\n\nThe companion test `bundled_skills_are_five_skill_directories_each_home_to_its_topics` runs `assert_home` for every skill. It is not needed for this subject.\n\nA failure would show up as the bootstrap skill missing its derivation content, or another skill restating its markers. The assertions would catch both. The check is phrase-based, which is a reasonable way to test \"stated in full in exactly one skill\". I found no unmet obligation, unnamed boundary case or missing marker.",
